---
/** How the text to speech tools work. */
import { VOICES, voiceDownloadBytes } from '@/lib/ml/voice/voices'

const size = Math.round(voiceDownloadBytes(VOICES[0]) / 1e6)
---

<h2>How to use it</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Type or paste English text, up to 20,000 characters.</li>
  <li>Choose a voice and a speed.</li>
  <li>Press Read aloud. The first time, the voice model downloads once ({size} MB).</li>
  <li>Listen, then download WAV or MP3.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>
  The tool runs Kokoro, an 82 million parameter speech model (Apache 2.0 licence), in a background thread
  of this page. The text is first turned into words: numbers, money, times, percentages and common abbreviations are
  written out. Each word is then turned into speech sounds with a pronunciation dictionary of about 180,000 English words
  (US and UK lexicons, Apache 2.0). A word that is not in it is built from known parts, such as
  "keyboards" from "key", "board" and "-s", or sounded out letter by letter. The model reads the sounds and a voice style
  and writes the audio at 24 kHz, a few sentences at a time.
</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Text</th><th>Read as</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
